[Music]  every three years we revalue your  property and update your rating  valuation or RV the RV is what we  estimate your property will sell for on  the 1st of August this year the RV isn't  a market value and shouldn't be used for  insurance or property sales that's  purely for the purpose of rates it's  important to know that an increase in  your property value doesn't necessarily  mean you'll pay more and rates to  explain that we need to look at how  rates are set rates are just one source  of council income and their level  depends on funding decisions made by the  council property value only makes up a  portion of your rates  it doesn't affect fixed charges rates  based on land area or targeted rates  such as the curbside collection service  you chose revaluation doesn't increase  the total amount that council collects  from rates instead that helps us work  out everyone's share if your property  has a lower than average valuation  increase it's likely to have no effect  on your rates and in some cases may mean  you pay less while a higher than average  valuation increase could result in  higher rates we won't know how your new  property value will impact your rates  until the start of the next rates year  you can object to your rating valuation  and details on how to do this will be on  the notices sync to property owners

